Let the side length of the regular hexagon be $s$.
The area of the smaller hexagon can be found by subtracting the areas of the triangles $AHG$, $BIH$, $CJI$, $DKJ$, $ELK$, and $FLG$ from the area of the regular hexagon $ABCDEF$.
The area of each triangle is half the base times the height, so
\begin{align*} [ABCDEF] &= 6 \cdot \frac{1}{2} \cdot s \cdot \frac{s\sqrt{3}}{2} = \frac{3s^2\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\ [AHG] &= [BIH] = [CJI] = [DKJ] = [ELK] = [FLG] = 6 \cdot \frac{1}{2} \cdot \frac{s}{2} \cdot \frac{s\sqrt{3}}{2} = \frac{3s^2\sqrt{3}}{8}. \end{align*}
Thus, the area of the smaller hexagon is
\[[ABCDEF] - 6[AHG] = \frac{3s^2\sqrt{3}}{2} - 6 \cdot \frac{3s^2\sqrt{3}}{8} = \frac{9s^2\sqrt{3}}{8}.\]
The ratio of the area of the smaller hexagon to the area of the regular hexagon is therefore $\frac{\frac{9s^2\sqrt{3}}{8}}{\frac{3s^2\sqrt{3}}{2}} = \frac{3}{8}$, and $m + n = 3 + 8 = \boxed{11}$.The answer is: 11
